The **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** is an open standard that lets AI applications discover and use external capabilities: your database, your task list, your internal APIs. Instead of writing a custom integration for every AI client, you build one MCP server, and any MCP-compatible client (Claude, IDE assistants, agent frameworks) can use it.

To learn it properly, I built [TaskPilot](https://github.com/shuklaharsh2809/mcp-server), a deliberately small task manager whose tasks are available both through a REST API and through an MCP server. This post explains the ideas and walks through the server code.

## MCP vs REST: you probably need both

The most common confusion about MCP is that it replaces REST. It doesn't. They serve different callers:

- **REST** serves conventional clients, like a React UI, that know exactly which endpoint to call.
- **MCP** serves AI hosts. A model doesn't know your endpoints in advance, so MCP gives it a standard way to **discover** what is available, read descriptions, and **invoke** capabilities with validated input.

In TaskPilot both sit on top of the same service and database layers:

```
React UI ───────────────→ REST API ──┐
                                     ├─→ TaskService → TaskRepository → SQLite
User → LLM → MCP Client → MCP Server ┘
```

That shared service layer is the key design decision. Business rules live in one place, and MCP is just another way in.

## The three things an MCP server exposes

1. **Tools**: actions the model can call, like `create_task` or `update_task`. Each has a description and an input schema.
2. **Resources**: read-only data the client can load into context, addressed by URIs like `tasks://pending`.
3. **Prompts**: reusable prompt templates the user can pick, like "review my tasks".

## Step 1: Create the server

TaskPilot uses the v2 TypeScript SDK packages. (Older tutorials use `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`; the ideas are the same.)

```ts
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server";
import { serveStdio } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server/stdio";
import * as z from "zod/v4";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "taskpilot", version: "1.0.0" });
```

## Step 2: Register a tool

A tool is a name, a description written **for the model**, an input schema, and a handler:

```ts
server.registerTool(
  "create_task",
  {
    description:
      "Create a task. Use when the user asks to add, create, schedule, or remember work.",
    inputSchema: z.object({
      title: z.string().trim().min(1).max(200),
      priority: z.enum(["low", "medium", "high"]).optional(),
      dueDate: z.string().regex(/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/).optional(),
    }),
  },
  async (input) => {
    const task = taskService.createTask(input);
    return {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: JSON.stringify(task, null, 2) }],
      structuredContent: task,
    };
  },
);
```

Two details matter more than they look:

- **The description is a prompt.** "Use when the user asks to add, create, schedule, or remember work" tells the model *when* to call the tool, not just what it does. Vague descriptions lead to tools being called at the wrong time.
- **The schema is your security boundary.** Model output is untrusted input. Zod rejects anything that doesn't match before your service ever sees it.

For failures, return an error result the model can read and recover from, instead of throwing:

```ts
return {
  content: [{ type: "text", text: JSON.stringify({ error: `Task '${id}' was not found.` }) }],
  isError: true,
};
```

## Step 3: Expose read-only data as resources

Resources are perfect for "give the model context" without granting it the power to change anything:

```ts
server.registerResource(
  "Pending Tasks",
  "tasks://pending",
  { title: "Pending Tasks", description: "Todo and in-progress tasks.", mimeType: "application/json" },
  async (uri) => ({
    contents: [
      {
        uri: uri.href,
        mimeType: "application/json",
        text: JSON.stringify(taskService.listTasks().filter((task) => task.status !== "completed")),
      },
    ],
  }),
);
```

## Step 4: Add a prompt template

```ts
server.registerPrompt(
  "review_tasks",
  {
    title: "Review Task List",
    description: "Review tasks for overdue work, priorities, duplicates and next actions.",
    argsSchema: z.object({ focus: z.string().trim().optional() }),
  },
  ({ focus }) => ({
    messages: [
      {
        role: "user",
        content: {
          type: "text",
          text: `Review these tasks${focus ? `, focusing on ${focus}` : ""}. Do not modify anything unless I ask.\n\n${JSON.stringify(taskService.listTasks(), null, 2)}`,
        },
      },
    ],
  }),
);
```

Note the "do not modify anything unless I ask" line. Prompts are a good place to set the behavior you expect.

## Step 5: Run it over stdio

```ts
serveStdio(() => server);
console.error("MCP server is waiting for a client on stdio.");
```

Log to `stderr`, never `stdout`: over the stdio transport, `stdout` carries the protocol messages, and stray logs there break the connection.

## Testing and connecting a client

Use the MCP Inspector to call your tools by hand before involving a model:

```bash
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector node dist/mcp/server.js
```

Then register the server with an MCP client. For Claude Desktop, add it to the config file:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "taskpilot": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/dist/mcp/server.js"]
    }
  }
}
```

## Security checklist for MCP servers

- Validate every tool input with a schema; treat model output like user input.
- Prefer **resources** for reading and keep write tools narrow and explicit.
- Make destructive tools (delete, send, pay) obvious in their names and descriptions so clients can ask for confirmation.
- Keep secrets in the server's environment, never in tool results.

## Key takeaways

- MCP standardizes how AI clients discover and call your capabilities; REST still serves your normal app.
- Put business logic in a shared service layer and expose it through both.
- Tool descriptions guide the model, schemas protect your system.
- Use resources for read-only context and prompts for reusable workflows.

The full working example, with a REST API, React UI and an optional agent CLI, is on GitHub: [shuklaharsh2809/mcp-server](https://github.com/shuklaharsh2809/mcp-server).

## FAQ

### Is MCP only for Claude?

No. MCP is an open protocol, and many AI clients, IDEs and agent frameworks support it. A single MCP server works with all of them.

### What's the difference between an MCP tool and a resource?

A tool performs an action and can change state. A resource is read-only data the client can load into the model's context.

### Do I need to rewrite my API to support MCP?

No. Keep your API and add an MCP server that calls the same service layer. That's exactly how TaskPilot is structured.
